<template>
    <SiteShell page="authoring">
        <div class="page-head">
            <h1>How an agent builds a deck</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                “Make a beautiful presentation” is a workflow, not a prompt. The
                agent works through seven stages, and each stage leaves evidence.
            </p>
        </div>

        <ol class="steps">
            <li>
                <h2>Clarify the brief</h2>
                <p>
                    Audience, purpose, duration, source material, language and
                    brand requirements. Missing facts are asked for, not invented.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h2>Plan the argument</h2>
                <p>
                    The claim of every page and the order of pages are settled
                    before any layout is drawn.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h2>Choose a visual direction</h2>
                <p>
                    Theme, master and semantic layouts are selected so the deck
                    looks like one system rather than a pile of slides.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h2>Generate editable objects</h2>
                <p>
                    Native charts, tables, formulas and shapes are preferred.
                    A whole page is never rasterised to dodge an editing problem.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h2>Review the whole deck</h2>
                <p>
                    A contact sheet and per-page previews are rendered. Fonts,
                    overflow, contrast, alignment and missing assets are checked.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h2>Revise locally</h2>
                <p>
                    Fixes address one object id at one revision. Changing one
                    chart does not rewrite the rest of the project.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h2>Deliver and verify</h2>
                <p>
                    Export, reopen the result and re-run the checks before the
                    deck is handed over.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <section class="section">
            <h2>What automatic checks cannot do</h2>
            <p>
                Rule checks catch measurable geometry and asset problems. Taste,
                narrative rhythm and whether a chart actually supports the claim
                still require looking at the pages. A single “beauty score” is
                not evidence.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <h2>Skills ship with the product</h2>
            <p>
                The <code>@wae/open-ppt-skills</code> package teaches agents to
                create, revise, fill brand templates, review and deliver decks
                through the real SDK and CLI. It is installed and validated with
                a clean setup, and it declares the SDK version it was built for.
            </p>
        </section>

        <p class="cta-line">
            Curious what is already implemented?
            <Link to="RoadmapPage">
                Check the roadmap
            </Link>
        </p>
    </SiteShell>
</template>

